[Term]
id: GO:0003674
name: molecular_function
def: "A molecular process that can be carried out by the action of a single macromolecular machine, usually via direct physical interactions with other molecular entities. Function in this sense denotes an action, or activity, that a gene product (or a complex) performs." [GOC:pdt]
is_a: BFO:0000015 ! process

[Term]
id: GO:0008150
name: biological_process
namespace: biological_process
def: "A biological process is the execution of a genetically-encoded biological module or program. It consists of all the steps required to achieve the specific biological objective of the module. A biological process is accomplished by a particular set of molecular functions carried out by specific gene products (or macromolecular complexes), often in a highly regulated manner and in a particular temporal sequence." [GOC:pdt]
xref: Wikipedia:Biological_process
is_a: BFO:0000015 ! process

[Term]
id: GO:0009987
name: cellular process
namespace: biological_process
def: "Any process that is carried out at the cellular level, but not necessarily restricted to a single cell. For example, cell communication occurs among more than one cell, but occurs at the cellular level." [GOC:curators, GOC:isa_complete]
is_a: GO:0008150 ! biological_process

[Term]
id: GO:0016301
name: kinase activity
def: "Catalysis of the transfer of a phosphate group, usually from ATP, to a substrate molecule." [ISBN:0198506732]
xref: Reactome:R-HSA-6788855 "FN3KRP phosphorylates PsiAm, RibAm"
xref: Reactome:R-HSA-6788867 "FN3K phosphorylates ketosamines"
is_a: GO:0003674 ! molecular_function

[Term]
id: GO:0022406
name: membrane docking
namespace: biological_process
def: "The initial attachment of a membrane or protein to a target membrane. Docking requires only that the proteins come close enough to interact and adhere." [GOC:isa_complete, PMID:27875684]
is_a: GO:0009987 ! cellular process

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000001
name: root of drug metabolism
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The human gut microbiome transforms and degrade drugs using enzymes." []
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000002
name: drug
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A drug is any substance that causes a change in an organism's physiology or psychology when consumed or introduced." []
is_a: HMDM:0000001 ! root of drug metabolism
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000005
name: antiviral
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "An agent that kills a virus or that suppresses its ability to replicate and, hence, inhibits its capability to multiply and reproduce. For example, amantadine (Symmetrel) is a synthetic antiviral. It acts by inhibiting the multiplication of the influenza A virus." [PMID:33726557]
is_a: HMDM:0000073 ! drug class
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000006
name: determinant of drug metabolism
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The primary factors that determine the drug metabolism." []
is_a: HMDM:0000001 ! root of drug metabolism
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000007
name: mechanism of drug metabolism
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The main mechanisms of drug modification, inactivation of a drug, and drug degradation." []
is_a: HMDM:0000001 ! root of drug metabolism
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000008
name: classification
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The categories to group enzymes into gene families, mechanisms, and drug classes." []
is_a: HMDM:0000001 ! root of drug metabolism
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000009
name: nucleoside antiviral
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Among the molecules directly targeting viruses, nucleoside analogues form an essential class of broad-spectrum antiviral drugs. Many direct-acting antiviral nucleoside analogues target the viral polymerase responsible for the viral genome replication. Unlike non-nucleoside polymerase inhibitors that often bind to allosteric non-conserved and mutation-tolerant sites, nucleoside analogues directly bind to the more conserved active site of the viral polymerase after conversion to their triphosphate active form. Nucleoside analogs represent the largest class of small molecule-based antivirals, which currently form the backbone of chemotherapy of chronic infections caused by HIV, hepatitis B or C viruses, and herpes viruses." [PMID:33924302]
is_a: HMDM:0000005 ! antiviral
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000010
name: Escherichia coli thymidine phosphorylase (deoA)
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The enzymes which catalyze the reversible phosphorolysis of pyrimidine nucleosides are involved in the degradation of these compounds and in their utilization as carbon and energy sources, or in the rescue of pyrimidine bases for nucleotide synthesis." [PMID:32526207, PMID:34260950]
is_a: HMDM:0000089 ! fluoropyrimidines deglycosylation enzyme
relationship: MOP:0001162 CHEBI:31521 ! deglycosylation doxifluridine
