[Term]
id: HMDM:0000021
name: central nervous system agent
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The central nervous system agents are medicines that affect the central nervous system (CNS). There are many different types of drugs that work on the CNS, including anesthetics, anticonvulsants, antiemetics, antiparkinson agents, CNS stimulants, muscle relaxants, narcotic analgesics (pain relievers), nonnarcotic analgesics (such as acetaminophen and NSAIDs), and sedatives. Features of CNS drugs are related to their ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier affinity and exhibit CNS activity and can be used in the management of neuro degenerative and psychiatric problems." [PMID:16489364, PMID:27439371]
is_a: HMDM:0000073 ! drug class
is_a: HMDM:0000488 ! clinical use
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000022
name: dopamine precursor
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A chemical compound that participates in a chemical reaction that produces dopamine." []
is_a: HMDM:0000021 ! central nervous system agent
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000023
name: Enterococcus faecalis tyrosine decarboxylase (TDC)
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Tyrosine decarboxylase (TDC) is an enzyme with the ability to decarboxylate L-tyrosine into tyramine. Due to the structural similarities between tyrosine and levodopa, TDC found in small intestinal bacteria can convert levodopa to dopamine. Moreover, commonly used decarboxylase inhibitors, carbidopa, benserazide, and methyldopa, do not inhibit TDC. This reduces the efficacy of the drug since the conversion of levodopa to dopamine occurs outside the brain." [PMID:25529314, PMID:30659181, PMID:31196984]
is_a: BFO:0000002 ! continuant
is_a: HMDM:0000092 ! carboxyl-lyase
relationship: MI:1140 CHEBI:15765 ! L-dopa
relationship: RO:0000056 HMDM:0000125 ! participates in decarboxylation of a molecule
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000025
name: cardiac therapeutic
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A group of therapeutics targeting the heart." []
is_a: HMDM:0000073 ! drug class
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000032
name: cardiac glycoside reductase (cgr) operon
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A specific bacterial enzyme that allows E. lenta bacteria to metabolize and deactivate digoxin." [PMID:23869020]
is_a: HMDM:0000095 ! Cytochrome c-type protein
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000035
name: Eggerthella lenta cgr1
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "This gene is responsible for encoding proteins which are homologous to bacterial cytochromes, this allows the bacteria to use digoxin as an alternate electron acceptor.  Cgr1 is not directly involved in digoxin reduction, instead, it has a more general function such as electron transfer, or membrane docking. The function of cgr1 is thought to be completing the electron transfer from a membrane-associated electron donor (e.g. the membrane quinone pool) to the [4Fe-4S] cluster of the Cgr2 reductase via its covalently bound heme groups. It is highly up-regulated by digoxin and other cardiac glycosides containing unsaturated butyrolactone rings, such as digitoxin, digoxigenin, and, to a lesser extent, ouabain. It is repressed by arginine but not by ornithine. It is a part of an operon that consists of cgr1 and cgr2." [PMID:21304654, PMID:23869020]
is_a: HMDM:0000095 ! Cytochrome c-type protein
relationship: MOP:0000615 HMDM:0000037 ! electron transfer Eggerthella lenta cgr2
relationship: RO:0000050 HMDM:0000032 ! cardiac glycoside reductase (cgr) operon
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000037
name: Eggerthella lenta cgr2
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "This gene is involved in the inactivation of the cardiac medication and plant natural product digoxin, thus decreasing drug efficacy and toxicity. It catalyzes the reduction of the alpha,beta-unsaturated butyrolactone ring of digoxin to the inactive metabolite dihydrodigoxin. It likely uses the cytochrome Cgr1 as the physiological electron donor, encoded by the adjacent gene in the locus. cgr2 only reduces digoxin and other cardenolide toxins, such as digitoxin, digoxigenin, ouabain and ouabagenin. Therefore, it is a specialized enzyme present in some gut bacteria E. lenta that protects their human host against ingested plant toxins." [PMID:21304654, PMID:23869020, PMID:29761785]
is_a: HMDM:0000095 ! Cytochrome c-type protein
relationship: MOP:0000569 CHEBI:28544 ! reduction digitoxin
relationship: MOP:0000569 CHEBI:42098 ! reduction digoxigenin
relationship: MOP:0000569 CHEBI:4551 ! reduction digoxin
relationship: RO:0000050 HMDM:0000032 ! cardiac glycoside reductase (cgr) operon
relationship: RO:0000056 HMDM:0000142 ! participates in reduction of a molecule
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000049
name: Beta-glucuronidase
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Beta-glucuronidases are members of the glycosidase family of enzymes that catalyze breakdown of complex carbohydrates. Bacterial beta-glucuronidase can metabolize to 18-beta-glycyrrhetinic acid (GA) as a main product and to 18-beta-glycyrrhetinic acid-3-O-beta-D-glucuronide (GAMG) as a minor product." [PMID:28578872]
is_a: HMDM:0000493 ! Glycoside hydrolase family 2
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000050
name: Escherichia coli  uridine phosphorylase (udp)
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The uridine phosphorylase catalyzes the reversible conversion of uridine to uracil and ribose-1-phosphate in bacteria. This enzyme efficiently deglycosylates capecitabine, deoxyuridine, and doxifluridine." [PMID:32526207, PMID:34260950]
is_a: HMDM:0000089 ! fluoropyrimidines deglycosylation enzyme
relationship: MOP:0001162 CHEBI:16450 ! deglycosylation 2'-deoxyuridine
relationship: MOP:0001162 CHEBI:31521 ! deglycosylation doxifluridine
relationship: RO:0000056 HMDM:0000126 ! participates in deglycosylation of a molecule
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000068
name: pyrimidine 5'-deoxyribonucleoside
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A pyrimidine deoxyribonucleoside that is a pyrimidine ribonucleoside in which the hydroxy group at the 5' position is replaced by a hydrogen." []
is_a: HMDM:0000009 ! nucleoside antiviral
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000073
name: drug class
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A drug class is a set of medications and other compounds that have similar chemical structures, the same mechanism of action, a related mode of action, and/or are used to treat the same disease." []
is_a: HMDM:0000008 ! classification
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000074
name: herbal medication and supplementation
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Products made from botanicals, or plants, that are used to treat diseases or to maintain health are called herbal products, botanical products, or phytomedicines. A product made from plants and used solely for internal use is called an herbal supplement." []
is_a: HMDM:0000073 ! drug class
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000076
name: anti-neoplastic agent
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Antineoplastic drugs (ANDs), also known as cytostatic drugs, are a heterogeneous group of chemicals used to control the growth of neoplastic cells. Most ANDs interfere with cell proliferation through DNA destruction, RNA synthesis, or duplication process. Unfortunately, ANDs do not distinguish cancer cells from normal ones, leading to unwanted adverse effects in patients receiving chemotherapy. The International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) classifies some ANDs as human carcinogens." [PMID:27110842]
is_a: HMDM:0000073 ! drug class
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000077
name: topoisomerase inhibitor
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Topoisomerase inhibitors are chemical compounds that block the action of topoisomerases. Topoisomerase plays key roles in cellular reproduction and DNA organization, as they facilitate the cleavage of single and double stranded DNA to relax supercoils, untangle catenanes, and condense chromosomes in eukaryotic cells. Topoisomerase inhibitors affect these essential cellular processes by preventing topoisomerases from performing DNA strand breaks or prevent the re-ligation step of the topoisomerase mechanism by interacting with topoisomerase-DNA complexes. These topoisomerase-DNA-inhibitor complexes are cytotoxic agents because the un-repaired single and double stranded DNA breaks that they cause can lead to apoptosis and cell death. Due to this ability to induce apoptosis, topoisomerase inhibitors have gained interest as therapeutics against infectious and cancerous cells." [PMID:22108601, PMID:29363591]
is_a: HMDM:0000076 ! anti-neoplastic agent
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000078
name: Camptothecin Analogues
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Camptothecin (CPT) is a topoisomerase inhibitor. It was discovered in 1966 during a systematic screening of natural products for anticancer drugs. It was isolated from the bark and stem of Camptotheca acuminata (Camptotheca, Happy tree), a tree native to China used in traditional Chinese medicine. CPT has showed anticancer activity in clinical trials, especially against breast, ovarian, colon, lung, and stomach cancers. To maximize the therapeutic effects, four CPT analogues have been approved and are used as antineoplastic agents." [PMID:21051639]
is_a: HMDM:0000077 ! topoisomerase inhibitor
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000081
name: irinotecan
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A member of the class of pyranoindolizinoquinolines that is the carbamate ester obtained by formal condensation of the carboxy group of [1,4'-bipiperidine]-1'-carboxylic acid with the phenolic hydroxy group of (4S)-4,11-diethyl-4,9-dihydroxy-1H-pyrano[3',4':6,7]indolizino[1,2- hydrochloride]quinoline-3,14-dione. Used (in the form of its hydrochloride salt trihydrate) in combination with fluorouracil and leucovorin, for the treatment of patients with metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pancreas after disease progression following gemcitabine-based therapy. It is converted via hydrolysis of the carbamate linkage to its active metabolite, SN-38, which is ~1000 times more active." []
is_a: HMDM:0000002 ! drug
is_a: HMDM:0000078 ! Camptothecin Analogues
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000089
name: fluoropyrimidines deglycosylation enzyme
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "These are enzymes responsible for the breakdown and utilization of nucleosides." []
is_a: BFO:0000002 ! continuant
is_a: HMDM:0000090 ! thymidine pyrimidine-nucleoside phosphorylase family
relationship: RO:0000056 HMDM:0000016 ! participates in antiviral inactivation
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000090
name: thymidine pyrimidine-nucleoside phosphorylase family
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Thymidine phosphorylase (TP) enzyme catalyzes the phosphorolysis of the nucleosides thymidine (dThd) and deoxyuridine (dUrd) to the corresponding bases thymine and uracil, and deoxyribose-1-phosphate. Thymidine phosphorylase (TP), is also related to Pyrimidine nucleoside phosphorylase (PYNP), which catalyzes the reversible phosphorolysis of pyrimidines in the nucleotide synthesis salvage pathway. In lower organisms (e.g. Bacillus stearothermophilus) PYNP accepts both thymidine and uridine, whereas in mammalian and other higher organisms it is specific for thymidine (designated thymidine phosphorylase, TP). PYNP shares 40% sequence similarity (and presumably significant structural similarity) with human TP." [PMID:27004974, PMID:9817849]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000091
name: Trastuzumab deruxtecan
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Trastuzumab deruxtecan is a monoclonal antibody medication used for the treatment of breast cancer or for the treatment of gastric or gastroesophageal adenocarcinoma. It is an antibody-drug conjugate composed of an anti-HER2 (human epidermal growth factor receptor 2) antibody, a cleavable tetrapeptide-based linker, and a cytotoxic topoisomerase I inhibitor." []
is_a: HMDM:0000002 ! drug
is_a: HMDM:0000078 ! Camptothecin Analogues
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000092
name: carboxyl-lyase
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "An enzyme that participates in a chemical reaction that removes a carboxyl group, decarboxylation, from a compound." []
is_a: HMDM:0000094 ! lyases family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000094
name: lyases family
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Lyases are enzymes that catalyze the bond construction and breaking by means other than hydrolysis or redox reactions. Such enzymes are particular in the sense that they only require one substrate for the reaction in one direction, but two substrates for the reverse reaction." [PMID:21417217]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
is_a: HMDM:0000896 ! Drug inactivation enzyme
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000095
name: Cytochrome c-type protein
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "These proteins are part of cardiac glycoside reductase (CGR) pathway. The CGR is responsible for reductive metabolism of cardiac glycoside. The expression of these enzymes can be inhibited by dietary arginine." [PMID:24637603]
is_a: BFO:0000002 ! continuant
is_a: HMDM:0000097 ! cytochrome protein family
relationship: RO:0000056 HMDM:0000098 ! participates in cardiac therapy metabolism
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000097
name: cytochrome protein family
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Cytochrome (CYP) enzymes catalyze the metabolic reactions of endogenous and exogenous compounds. The superfamily of enzymes is found across many organisms, regardless of type, except for plants. Differences in cytochrome subunits are expected to be critical in deciding the unique substrates that allow this functional diversity." []
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000098
name: cardiac therapy metabolism
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The process of breaking down of a cardiac therapy drug which improve the heart's energy production and efficiency." []
is_a: HMDM:0000007 ! mechanism of drug metabolism
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000099
name: sulindac
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A monocarboxylic acid that is 1-benzylidene-1H-indene which is substituted at positions 2, 3, and 5 by methyl, carboxymethyl, and fluorine respectively, and in which the phenyl group of the benzylidene moiety is substituted at the para position by a methylsulfinyl group. It is a prodrug for the corresponding sulfide, a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug, used particularly in the treatment of acute and chronic inflammatory conditions. Sulindac is used to relieve pain, tenderness, swelling, and stiffness caused by osteoarthritis (arthritis caused by a breakdown of the lining of the joints), rheumatoid arthritis (arthritis caused by swelling of the lining of the joints), and ankylosing spondylitis (arthritis that mainly affects the spine)." []
is_a: HMDM:0000002 ! drug
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000108
name: raloxifene
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "A member of the class of 1-benzothiophenes that is 1-benzothiophene in which the hydrogens at positions 2, 3, and 6 have been replaced by p-hydroxyphenyl, p-[2-(piperidin-1-yl)ethoxy]benzoyl, and hydroxy groups, respectively. Raloxifene is a medication used to prevent and treat osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and those on glucocorticoids. It is also used to reduce the risk of breast cancer in those at high risk. Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) and therefore a mixed agonist–antagonist of the estrogen receptor (ER). It has estrogenic effects in bone and antiestrogenic effects in the breasts and uterus." []
is_a: HMDM:0000002 ! drug
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

