property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000493
name: Glycoside hydrolase family 2
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Glycoside hydrolases (GHs) (EC 3.2.1.X) hydrolyze glycosidic bonds between carbohydrates or between carbohydrate and non-carbohydrate components, for instance alcohols or phenols. To date, enzyme classification is based on amino acid similarities rather than substrate specificity. GHs are assigned to different groups based on conserved domains, and the families are numbered from 1 to 131 that are listed in the Carbohydrate-Active Enzymes Database. Glycoside hydrolases classified as GH2 were described to function as beta-galactosidase (EC 3.2.1.23) ; beta-mannosidase (EC 3.2.1.25); beta-glucuronidase (EC 3.2.1.31); alpha-L-arabinofuranosidase (EC 3.2.1.55); mannosylglycoprotein endo-beta-mannosidase (EC 3.2.1.152); exo-beta-glucosaminidase (EC 3.2.1.165); alpha-L-arabinopyranosidase (EC 3.2.1.-); beta-galacturonidase (EC 3.2.1.-); beta-xylosidase (EC 3.2.1.37); beta-D-galactofuranosidase (EC 3.2.1.146); beta-glucosidase (EC 3.2.1.21); beta-galacturonidase RGII specific (EC 3.2.1.-); glycyrrhizin beta-glucuronidase (EC 3.2.1.128). These enzymes hydrolyze their substrates through a retaining acid/base mechanism with two glutamic acid/glutamate residues involved. One residue acts as acid and base catalyst, whereas the other residue serves as nucleophile." [PMID:26090361]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000494
name: Endoglucanase E precursor (EGE)
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "There are many subtypes of endoglucanases (e.g., endoglucanase I, II) each with its own molecular topology with some organisms capable of producing several different subtypes of endoglucanases. As a whole these endoglucanases are grouped into glycoside hydrolase families (glycoside hydrolase 5, 6, etc.). Endoglucanases vary in their substrate specificity being able to cleave differing glycosidic bonds (beta-1-4, beta-1-6, etc.).The Endoglucanase E (EGE) enzyme has a wide substrate specificity and cleaves beta1,4- and beta1,3- glycosidic linkages in addition to exhibiting activity on beta1,4- xylosidic bonds. The primary structure of EGE also has several unusual and notable features. BAL31 studies revealed that 300 C-terminal residues including those of the conserved region can be deleted from the enzyme without loss of catalytic activity. An endoglucanase E precursor would be a physiologically inactive substance that could at one point through a process be converted into endoglucanase E precursor." [PMID:1366808, PMID:29856054]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000495
name: phosphorylation of a molecule
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Enzymatic phosphorylation of a molecule" []
is_a: HMDM:0000015 ! mechanism
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000496
name: Purine nucleoside phosphorylase
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Purine nucleoside phosphorylases (PNPs, E. C. 2.4.2.1) use orthophosphate to cleave the N-glycosidic bond of beta-(deoxy)ribonucleosides to yield alpha-(deoxy)ribose 1-phosphate and the free purine base. Purine nucleoside phosphorylase (PNP) catalyzes a reversible phosphorolysis of purine deoxy- and ribonucleosides with formation of (d)Rib-1-P and appropriate bases. PNP plays a leading role in the cell metabolism of nucleosides and nucleotides, as well as in maintaining the immune status of an organism. The major purpose of the majority of studies on the PNP is the detection of high-performance enzyme inhibitors, derivatives of the purine nucleosides, which are used in medicine as immunosuppressors." [PMID:15808857, PMID:19248622]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000497
name: 20beta-Hydroxysteroid Dehydrogenase
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The bacterial steroid-17,20-desmolase pathway cleaves the cortisol side chain, forming pro-androgens predicted to impact host physiology. Bacterial 20beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (20beta-HSDH) regulates cortisol side-chain cleavage by reducing the C-20 carboxyl group on cortisol, yielding 20beta-dihydrocortisol." [PMID:31209107]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000499
name: NADH dehydrogenase
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Non-proton pumping type II NADH dehydrogenase (NDH-2) plays a central role in the respiratory metabolism of bacteria, and in the mitochondria of fungi, plants and protists. The lack of NDH-2 in mammalian mitochondria and its essentiality in important bacterial pathogens suggests these enzymes may represent a potential new drug target to combat microbial pathogens. NADH dehydrogenases ensures adequate oxidation of NADH to NAD+. Most species possess one to two isozymes of the non-proton-translocating type 2 dehydrogenase (Ndh), also termed alternative NADH dehydrogenase. While complex I is deeply integrated into the inner membrane, alternative NADH dehydrogenases are not membrane-embedded, but rather associated to the membrane surface, either on the IMS side (external NADH dehydrogenases) or the matrix side (internal NADH dehydrogenases). Unlike complex I, alternative NADH dehydrogenase lack flavin mononucleotide (FMN) and iron-sulfur clusters as reactive groups. Instead, they contain a flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD) cofactor to mediate the electron transfer from NAD(P)H to ubiquinone. Alternative NADH dehydrogenase can serve as important valves on the respiratory chain when oxygen tensions suddenly rise. NADH dehydrogenases also serve as as ‘apoptosis inducing factors’ in a poorly understood process that involves the formation of a toxic protein fragment on the outside or inside of the mitochondrial outer membrane and in animals but not in yeast, AIFM1 serves as biogenesis factor for MIA40 and thus, indirectly, for many IMS proteins." [PMID:32245760, PMID:32769219]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000501
name: Dihydrodaidzein reductase
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Dihydrodaidzein reductase (DHDR), is a novel enzyme that belongs to the short-chain dehydrogenase/reductase (SDR) family, and it has a unique catalytic tetrad pattern (Asn, Ser, and HXXXK) that is somewhat different from that of general SDR enzymes (Asn, Ser, and YXXXK), while the alignment of the amino acid sequences of the NAD(P)H binding site is conserved. DHDR requires NADPH as a cofactor but not NADH, possibly explained by the presence of the arginine (R64) residue. In the case of DHDR from S. isoflavoniconvertens, the tyrosine of most of the known SDR family enzymes seems to be replaced with histidine, for which imidazole ring (de)protonation would be engaged in substrate reduction or oxidation. DHDR is involved in the reduction of (S)- and (R)-dihydrodaidzein, (S)- and (R)-DHD,  to trans- and cis-tetrahydrodaidzein (trans- and cis-THD), respectively." [PMID:26801575, PMID:27508112]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000503
name: Lysophospholipase L1 and related esterase
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Escherichia coli thioesterase I (TAP) is a versatile enzyme possessing activities of thioesterase, esterase, arylesterase, protease, and lysophospholipase. It has been documented with three different names, including thioesterase I, protease I and lysophospholipase L1, which are responsible for the degradation of thioester, amide, and ester bonds. E. coli thioesterase I, first identified by Barnes & Wakil, was cloned in 1993 and shown to catalyze hydrolytic cleavage of acyl-CoA thioesters. At the same time, protease I, named for its hydrolytic activity toward N-acetyl-dl-phenylalanine-2-naphthyl ester, was also cloned and characterized. Lysophospholipase L1, which catalyzes the hydrolysis of 1-acyl group of lysophospholipid was purified and its DNA sequence deduced in 1999. Recent studies showed that the gene sequences of E. coli thioesterase I (tesA), protease I (apeA) and lysophospholipase L1 (pldC) were identical and located at the same genomic position. Also, substrate competition assays showed that thioesterase I and protease I utilize the same active site, and all of them were identified as periplasmic proteins. The most plausible physiological role of TAP is that of lysophospholipase L1, since TAP shows specificity towards lysophospholipids and its activity on 1-acyl lysophosphatidylcholine follows typical Michaelis–Menten kinetics. TAP has stereoselectivity for amino acid derivative substrates, hence it is useful for the kinetic resolution of racemic mixtures of industrial chemicals." [PMID:12842470, PMID:4871199]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000707
name: Alpha/beta hydrolase superfamily
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "The core of each enzyme is similar: an alpha/beta sheet, not barrel, of eight beta-sheets connected by alpha-helices. These enzymes have diverged from a common ancestor so as to preserve the arrangement of the catalytic residues, not the binding site. They all have a catalytic triad, the elements of which are borne on loops which are the best-conserved structural features in the fold. The alpha/beta-hydrolase fold enzymes are one of the largest known protein families, including lipases, esterases, amidases, epoxide hydrolases, dehalogenases and hydroxynitrile lyases. The alpha/beta-hydrolase fold consists of eight beta-strands connected by alpha-helixes. These fold into a mostly parallel central beta-sheet surrounded by alpha-helixes. The catalytic triad consists of a nucleophile (serine, aspartate or cysteine), a histidine and a catalytic acid (aspartate or glutamate). These residues occur on conserved locations in loops and the alpha/beta-hydrolase fold brings them together to form the active site. Many alpha/beta-hydrolase fold enzymes also contain cap domains, the structures of which vary considerably between the different members. A cap domain sits on top of the active site, which is in the alpha/beta-hydrolase domain. In spite of their similar three-dimensional structures and similar active site residues, alpha/beta-hydrolase fold enzymes catalyse reactions with substrates containing a broad variety of functional groups, including carboxylic acid derivatives (esters, amides and thioesters), epoxides, alkyl halides and hydroxynitriles. The common mechanistic feature of these reactions is a nucleophilic attack." [PMID:1409539, PMID:21506229]
is_a: HMDM:0000012 ! gene family
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

[Term]
id: HMDM:0000708
name: statins
namespace: human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology
def: "Statins, inhibitors of the hydroxymethylglutaryl-CoA (HMG-CoA) reductase enzyme, are molecules of fungal origin. Statins are powerful cholesterol lowering medications and have provided outstanding contributions to the prevention of cardiovascular disease." [PMID:24657242, PMID:26975972]
is_a: HMDM:0000073 ! drug class
property_value: dcterms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280

